exercises.reference:
from functools import total_ordering


@total_ordering
class Version:
    def __init__(self, text):
        self.text = text
        self.parts = tuple(int(p) for p in text.split("."))

    def __repr__(self):
        return f"Version({self.text!r})"

    def __eq__(self, other):
        if not isinstance(other, Version):
            return NotImplemented
        return self.parts == other.parts

    def __hash__(self):
        return hash(self.parts)

    def __lt__(self, other):
        if not isinstance(other, Version):
            return NotImplemented
        return self.parts < other.parts

exercises.solution:
Parse the text once, in `__init__`, into a tuple of integers; tuples already compare lexicographically, element by element, and integers compare numerically, so `(1, 10, 0) > (1, 9, 3)`.

```python
from functools import total_ordering

@total_ordering
class Version:
    def __init__(self, text):
        self.text = text
        self.parts = tuple(int(p) for p in text.split("."))

    def __repr__(self):
        return f"Version({self.text!r})"

    def __eq__(self, other):
        if not isinstance(other, Version):
            return NotImplemented
        return self.parts == other.parts

    def __hash__(self):
        return hash(self.parts)

    def __lt__(self, other):
        if not isinstance(other, Version):
            return NotImplemented
        return self.parts < other.parts
```

`__hash__` hashes exactly what `__eq__` compares, so the hashing contract holds. `@total_ordering` derives `__le__`, `__gt__` and `__ge__`. Real projects use `packaging.version.Version`, which also understands pre-releases such as `2.0.0rc1`.
